00:26:19,502 --> 00:26:20,122
[SPEAKER_03]: It wasn't too long.

621
00:26:20,142 --> 00:26:21,023
[SPEAKER_03]: He was in the big time.

622
00:26:21,043 --> 00:26:21,743
[SPEAKER_03]: I think you're right.

623
00:26:22,023 --> 00:26:22,783
[SPEAKER_02]: He had a no-hitter.

624
00:26:24,004 --> 00:26:24,344
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

625
00:26:27,046 --> 00:26:27,446
[SPEAKER_03]: Well.

626
00:26:28,750 --> 00:26:31,792
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, Detroit had a great ball club that one year, too.

627
00:26:32,352 --> 00:26:39,736
[SPEAKER_03]: At York, and Greenberg, Rogale, Charlie Gerringer, one of the best second basemen that ever lived, I think.

628
00:26:39,916 --> 00:26:42,197
[SPEAKER_03]: He was my man, a man that I would look forward to.

629
00:26:42,817 --> 00:26:45,518
[SPEAKER_03]: And Ed Cochran, Catching, they had a good ball club over there.

630
00:26:46,039 --> 00:26:50,521
[SPEAKER_03]: And they had a fellow there so big he could wire that left field out when Grove was pitching, they said.

631
00:26:51,191 --> 00:26:52,071
[SPEAKER_03]: Bob Fothergate.

632
00:26:52,091 --> 00:26:53,112
[SPEAKER_02]: You remember him?

633
00:26:53,492 --> 00:26:56,292
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, he'd hit the way 300 pounds.

634
00:26:56,592 --> 00:26:59,853
[SPEAKER_03]: He'd hit line drives up in the left field.

635
00:27:00,453 --> 00:27:02,654
[SPEAKER_03]: Did he take very good care of himself off the field?

636
00:27:03,014 --> 00:27:04,634
[SPEAKER_03]: I just don't know a thing about him.

637
00:27:04,654 --> 00:27:05,855
[SPEAKER_03]: He's from Massillon, Ohio.

638
00:27:05,895 --> 00:27:06,855
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's right.

639
00:27:06,875 --> 00:27:07,455
[SPEAKER_03]: That's right.

640
00:27:08,675 --> 00:27:12,536
[SPEAKER_03]: I get a lot of cards and a lot of autographs now from people all over the country.

641
00:27:12,556 --> 00:27:13,096
[SPEAKER_03]: Is that right?

642
00:27:13,236 --> 00:27:13,776
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, sir.

643
00:27:13,796 --> 00:27:14,917
[SPEAKER_03]: I've got four or five of you now.

644
00:27:14,937 --> 00:27:16,977
[SPEAKER_03]: I've got to sign up and send to them.

645
00:27:18,277 --> 00:27:19,258
[SPEAKER_03]: I'd like to hear from them.

646
00:27:23,439 --> 00:27:28,103
[SPEAKER_02]: How'd you like playing in Comiskey Park with those long fences?

647
00:27:28,963 --> 00:27:31,125
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, I was never a home-run hitter.

648
00:27:31,205 --> 00:27:34,968
[SPEAKER_03]: I was mostly a hit-and-run man, and it didn't matter much about the long ball.

649
00:27:34,988 --> 00:27:41,553
[SPEAKER_03]: Now Simmons, when he came there, after he left athletics, he'd hit one back and they'd catch it right up against him.

650
00:27:41,573 --> 00:27:44,255
[SPEAKER_02]: He'd say, uh-oh, that'd been a home run in Philadelphia.

651
00:27:44,735 --> 00:27:45,736
[SPEAKER_03]: And it would have to.

652
00:27:46,156 --> 00:27:50,059
[SPEAKER_03]: You know, the wind, nine games out of ten, the wind's blowing in there.

653
00:27:50,773 --> 00:27:53,634
[SPEAKER_03]: And we always, between innings, we'd turn around.

654
00:27:53,814 --> 00:27:55,375
[SPEAKER_03]: There's a flag in center field.

655
00:27:56,035 --> 00:28:01,157
[SPEAKER_03]: We'd watch that flag and tell where the wind was blowing, where to go catch the ball.

656
00:28:01,297 --> 00:28:01,477
[SPEAKER_03]: I see.

657
00:28:01,997 --> 00:28:02,917
[SPEAKER_02]: Uh-huh.

658
00:28:02,937 --> 00:28:04,258
[SPEAKER_03]: Zeke went over up there in your time?

659
00:28:04,318 --> 00:28:05,178
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, sir.

660
00:28:05,298 --> 00:28:09,860
[SPEAKER_03]: Zeke used to take two steps off of first base and say, hey, Jackie boy, there's that line.

661
00:28:09,880 --> 00:28:10,940
[SPEAKER_03]: Don't you come over that line.

662
00:28:11,020 --> 00:28:11,821
[SPEAKER_03]: I'll catch that ball.

663
00:28:11,841 --> 00:28:14,182
[SPEAKER_03]: If you come over that line, I'll get everything inside that line.

664
00:28:14,822 --> 00:28:15,702
[SPEAKER_03]: That's about two steps.

665
00:28:15,722 --> 00:28:18,043
[SPEAKER_03]: And Zeke was a fine feller.

666
00:28:18,526 --> 00:28:20,527
[SPEAKER_03]: But you could totally see Cabal at first base.

667
00:28:21,008 --> 00:28:24,590
[SPEAKER_03]: He'd reach out to get it, and if his foot wasn't on the bag, the ball just went on by.

668
00:28:24,851 --> 00:28:28,093
[SPEAKER_03]: He wouldn't get off the bag and catch the ball and then tag the man, like Joe Koo.

669
00:28:28,153 --> 00:28:29,354
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, we had a great infield.

670
00:28:29,934 --> 00:28:32,396
[SPEAKER_03]: And Marvin Owen came over there, Luke Appling.

671
00:28:32,776 --> 00:28:34,517
[SPEAKER_03]: I played second base, and Joe Koo.

672
00:28:35,098 --> 00:28:37,059
[SPEAKER_03]: You remember Joe Koo used to be a watcher?

673
00:28:37,519 --> 00:28:38,560
[SPEAKER_03]: He was a first baseman.

674
00:28:38,800 --> 00:28:40,321
[SPEAKER_03]: We had a good ball.

675
00:28:40,362 --> 00:28:41,042
[SPEAKER_03]: Great infield.

676
00:28:41,502 --> 00:28:43,804
[SPEAKER_02]: defensively and feel one of the best in the country, I thought.

677
00:28:43,824 --> 00:28:45,465
[SPEAKER_02]: Zeke wasn't too mobile then, huh?

678
00:28:45,505 --> 00:28:47,947
[SPEAKER_02]: He just didn't... Zeke could hit that ball now.

679
00:28:48,288 --> 00:28:49,829
[SPEAKER_02]: He couldn't run, but he...

680
00:28:49,869 --> 00:28:50,910
[SPEAKER_02]: He could hit that ball.

681
00:28:51,010 --> 00:28:52,531
[SPEAKER_02]: He didn't have to run.

682
00:28:52,551 --> 00:28:55,093
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, he just let the ball go by one field and that was all.

683
00:28:55,634 --> 00:28:56,354
[SPEAKER_03]: Zeke was a wonder.

684
00:28:56,634 --> 00:28:57,055
[SPEAKER_02]: Uh-huh.

685
00:28:57,535 --> 00:28:59,036
[SPEAKER_02]: Henry Vanura.

686
00:28:59,076 --> 00:28:59,597
[SPEAKER_03]: Mm-hmm.

687
00:28:59,837 --> 00:29:01,098
[SPEAKER_02]: Quite a comedian, was he?

688
00:29:01,358 --> 00:29:07,924
[SPEAKER_03]: He walked in the hotel in, let's see, in Boston in the 90s there.

689
00:29:09,120 --> 00:29:10,200
[SPEAKER_03]: I want some watermelon.

690
00:29:10,220 --> 00:29:14,622
[SPEAKER_03]: He got him watermelon, walked in the lobby and the girls and the boys all dressed in the evening clothes.

691
00:29:14,642 --> 00:29:16,423
[SPEAKER_03]: They had a big ball there.

692
00:29:16,443 --> 00:29:18,824
[SPEAKER_03]: Zeke had a watermelon on his shoulder and had a red cap behind him.

693
00:29:18,844 --> 00:29:20,965
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm gonna get me some of that good New Orleans watermelon.

694
00:29:23,146 --> 00:29:23,886
[SPEAKER_03]: He was a wonder.

695
00:29:24,426 --> 00:29:25,567
[SPEAKER_03]: He had a bad leg.

696
00:29:27,608 --> 00:29:32,250
[SPEAKER_03]: Back then, where the broadcast from upstairs above Comiskey Park, they had to climb a ladder to get up there.

697
00:29:33,267 --> 00:29:38,070
[SPEAKER_03]: Zeke went up there and he kept talking, talking, and Dykes said, Zeke, you better get back in that ballgame.

698
00:29:38,090 --> 00:29:42,172
[SPEAKER_03]: He said, Papa, as soon as my groin gets well, I'll be ready to play.

699
00:29:42,192 --> 00:29:43,532
[SPEAKER_03]: He said, well, you better hurry up.

700
00:29:43,592 --> 00:29:45,453
[SPEAKER_03]: He said, let me tell you something.

701
00:29:45,533 --> 00:29:46,614
[SPEAKER_03]: Asked him two or three days later.

702
00:29:46,634 --> 00:29:48,135
[SPEAKER_03]: He said, now, Zeke, let me tell you something.

703
00:29:48,775 --> 00:29:57,579
[SPEAKER_03]: If you don't hurry up and get back in that ballgame, that train you've been talking about going down to good old New Orleans, if you don't hurry up and get back in that ballgame, you're going to be on that train going to New Orleans.

704
00:29:59,761 --> 00:30:00,841
[SPEAKER_03]: Dykes was a great man.

705
00:30:00,861 --> 00:30:02,082
[SPEAKER_03]: He knew who to pat on the back.

706
00:30:02,827 --> 00:30:05,049
[SPEAKER_03]: He knew who to kick around.

707
00:30:05,209 --> 00:30:07,070
[SPEAKER_03]: He just knew how to manage a ball club.

708
00:30:07,110 --> 00:30:07,771
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, sir.

709
00:30:08,812 --> 00:30:09,692
[SPEAKER_02]: How'd he handle you?

710
00:30:09,953 --> 00:30:11,174
[SPEAKER_02]: Did he need any help?

711
00:30:11,194 --> 00:30:11,834
[SPEAKER_02]: Didn't bother me.

712
00:30:12,174 --> 00:30:16,218
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, the man next to me, Luke Appling over there, aches and pains.

713
00:30:16,318 --> 00:30:18,940
[SPEAKER_03]: He'd start to holler, oh, my leg's hurting, my shin's hurting.

714
00:30:18,960 --> 00:30:20,041
[SPEAKER_03]: He'd get about five hits.

715
00:30:20,081 --> 00:30:21,682
[SPEAKER_03]: We were playing in Washington one day.

716
00:30:22,723 --> 00:30:25,305
[SPEAKER_03]: Somebody took his bat up in batting practice and broke it.

717
00:30:25,325 --> 00:30:27,266
[SPEAKER_03]: He hollered at the bench when he went up to hit.

718
00:30:27,326 --> 00:30:28,867
[SPEAKER_00]: Who broke my bat?

719
00:30:28,887 --> 00:30:30,048
[SPEAKER_03]: Who broke my bat?

720
00:30:30,068 --> 00:30:30,489
[SPEAKER_03]: Him, Luke.

721
00:30:30,809 --> 00:30:32,050
[SPEAKER_03]: Took a new one, went up there.

722
00:30:32,882 --> 00:30:34,023
[SPEAKER_03]: Sangled to right field.

723
00:30:34,323 --> 00:30:36,124
[SPEAKER_03]: Next time he went to bat, all of us on the bench.

724
00:30:36,565 --> 00:30:37,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Who broke Luke's bat?

725
00:30:38,266 --> 00:30:38,606
Pow!

726
00:30:38,786 --> 00:30:39,907
[SPEAKER_03]: Sangled to right field.

727
00:30:40,408 --> 00:30:41,508
[SPEAKER_03]: Five for five.

728
00:30:41,528 --> 00:30:45,331
[SPEAKER_03]: That year he hit .388 and led the league.

729
00:30:45,371 --> 00:30:47,793
[SPEAKER_03]: Dykes had all of us yell, who broke Luke's bat?

730
00:30:49,174 --> 00:30:51,716
[SPEAKER_02]: Did he really foul off all those pitches he didn't want to hit?

731
00:30:51,736 --> 00:30:52,136
[SPEAKER_03]: Purpose?

732
00:30:53,157 --> 00:30:53,838
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't think so.

733
00:30:53,878 --> 00:30:55,919
[SPEAKER_03]: But he had such a good eye.

734
00:30:56,800 --> 00:30:58,141
[SPEAKER_03]: There's a reason those balls are foul.

735
00:30:59,072 --> 00:31:03,576
[SPEAKER_03]: We'd get a big crowd out there, he'd start singing, I'd say, what in the world's the matter with you?

736
00:31:04,056 --> 00:31:05,498
[SPEAKER_03]: Before, when the ballgame started.

737
00:31:05,938 --> 00:31:08,420
[SPEAKER_03]: You are my sunshine, my pretty sunshine.

738
00:31:08,460 --> 00:31:10,722
[SPEAKER_03]: And he'd cut down the, I'd say, what in the world's the matter with you?

739
00:31:10,742 --> 00:31:11,943
[SPEAKER_03]: 40,000 people up here.

740
00:31:12,383 --> 00:31:13,685
[SPEAKER_03]: He said, I'm just unlaxing a little.

741
00:31:13,705 --> 00:31:15,626
[SPEAKER_03]: You are my sunshine, my pretty sunshine.

742
00:31:16,107 --> 00:31:17,688
[SPEAKER_03]: After the first inning was over, he was all right.

743
00:31:18,589 --> 00:31:20,330
[SPEAKER_03]: He sang, he cut down the old pine tree.

744
00:31:20,390 --> 00:31:22,152
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, you are my sunshine.

745
00:31:22,392 --> 00:31:24,174
[SPEAKER_03]: But, I said, now Luke, that's all right.

746
00:31:24,214 --> 00:31:25,715
[SPEAKER_03]: But one day he started singing.

747
00:31:26,294 --> 00:31:28,697
[SPEAKER_02]: Sweet little Arnie, if you remember that song.

748
00:31:28,937 --> 00:31:33,963
[SPEAKER_03]: If you heard him singing, when he got to that sweet little Arnie, I said, that has to stop.

749
00:31:35,084 --> 00:31:36,085
[SPEAKER_03]: He was a card.

750
00:31:36,425 --> 00:31:37,627
[SPEAKER_03]: He was a great ball player, though.

751
00:31:37,767 --> 00:31:38,588
[SPEAKER_03]: How about fielding?

752
00:31:38,628 --> 00:31:39,689
[SPEAKER_03]: Is he pretty good out there?

753
00:31:39,849 --> 00:31:40,570
[SPEAKER_03]: Who, Luke?

754
00:31:40,610 --> 00:31:41,291
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, sir.

755
00:31:41,491 --> 00:31:44,334
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, when he first came up, both of us kicked it all over the infield.

756
00:31:45,095 --> 00:31:47,838
[SPEAKER_02]: But after we got working together, it's fine.

757
00:31:48,313 --> 00:31:52,094
[SPEAKER_02]: How would you work your double plays, say you got a right-hand hitter coming up?

758
00:31:52,234 --> 00:31:54,015
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, it all depends on how you pitch to him.

759
00:31:54,335 --> 00:31:57,436
[SPEAKER_03]: If you're going to pitch him inside, naturally you shortstop cover.

760
00:31:58,036 --> 00:32:01,917
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you're going to pitch him outside of curveball, he might hit the right field.

761
00:32:02,297 --> 00:32:05,198
[SPEAKER_03]: I'd give a signal on each pitch as to who was to cover.

762
00:32:05,218 --> 00:32:06,859
[SPEAKER_03]: You'd wait and see what the pitcher did?

763
00:32:06,879 --> 00:32:09,580
[SPEAKER_03]: As soon as they called for the pitch, I'd give him the signal.

764
00:32:09,880 --> 00:32:11,140
[SPEAKER_03]: Open mouth, he was to cover.

765
00:32:11,640 --> 00:32:13,661
[SPEAKER_03]: Closed mouth, I was to cover.

766
00:32:15,006 --> 00:32:15,506
[SPEAKER_03]: That's right.

767
00:32:15,687 --> 00:32:17,948
[SPEAKER_03]: I often wondered what was being said.

768
00:32:17,988 --> 00:32:18,869
[SPEAKER_03]: We didn't say anything.

769
00:32:18,909 --> 00:32:19,790
[SPEAKER_03]: We just had to sign.

770
00:32:19,930 --> 00:32:23,673
[SPEAKER_03]: See, we'd come up, open mouth, you to cover, close mouth, I'm to cover.

771
00:32:25,094 --> 00:32:35,542
[SPEAKER_03]: And you'd go in and sometimes you'd tell the pitcher, too, if it was a man that hit always to left field, I'd holler to the pitcher, say, hey, Ted, give that ball to me, not second base.

772
00:32:36,683 --> 00:32:38,525
[SPEAKER_02]: Was it easy to work with Luke around second base?

773
00:32:38,545 --> 00:32:39,285
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, sir.

774
00:32:39,386 --> 00:32:39,706
[SPEAKER_03]: Great.

775
00:32:40,106 --> 00:32:40,907
[SPEAKER_03]: Great to work with him.

776
00:32:41,107 --> 00:32:42,328
[SPEAKER_02]: He was a good man to work with.

777
00:32:42,822 --> 00:32:45,804
[SPEAKER_02]: We led the league, I think, for second and double plays, yeah.

778
00:32:46,825 --> 00:32:48,287
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you ever take part in a triple play?

779
00:32:48,767 --> 00:32:49,147
[SPEAKER_03]: No, sir.

780
00:32:49,648 --> 00:32:49,868
[SPEAKER_03]: Never.

781
00:32:49,908 --> 00:32:50,829
[SPEAKER_03]: I didn't get close.

782
00:32:53,070 --> 00:32:54,872
[SPEAKER_03]: Did you ever hear of anybody named Smead Jolly?

783
00:32:56,173 --> 00:32:58,235
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, I got a funny one I want to tell you on Smead.

784
00:32:58,255 --> 00:33:00,196
[SPEAKER_03]: They all told it, so I guess I should.

785
00:33:01,938 --> 00:33:09,344
[SPEAKER_03]: In Boston, out in the left field, there used to be a dump, a hill up there, a mound up there, and then the fences up on that mound.

786
00:33:11,040 --> 00:33:13,321
[SPEAKER_03]: One day, Smead missed a couple of balls out there.

787
00:33:13,381 --> 00:33:14,782
[SPEAKER_03]: Donnie Bush, they say it now.

788
00:33:14,802 --> 00:33:15,603
[SPEAKER_03]: This is a story.

789
00:33:16,263 --> 00:33:21,186
[SPEAKER_03]: Took him out before the ball game and hit him balls up there, and he'd just catch them and catch them and catch them.

790
00:33:21,786 --> 00:33:27,990
[SPEAKER_03]: And then when the game started, someone hit a ball out there, and Smead just fell all down that bank field box.

791
00:33:28,670 --> 00:33:31,512
[SPEAKER_03]: Came in, Donnie got up and said, Jolly, I practiced you.

792
00:33:31,592 --> 00:33:33,273
[SPEAKER_03]: Why in the world do you catch that ball?

793
00:33:33,613 --> 00:33:36,214
[SPEAKER_03]: He said, well, you taught me how to go up the bank.

794
00:33:36,254 --> 00:33:37,755
[SPEAKER_03]: You didn't tell me how to come down.

795
00:33:38,095 --> 00:33:39,396
[SPEAKER_03]: They said he fell right on his face.

796
00:33:40,105 --> 00:33:41,606
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, that's the one they told him.

797
00:33:41,666 --> 00:33:42,126
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't know.

798
00:33:42,386 --> 00:33:44,047
[SPEAKER_02]: How about the three errors in one play?

799
00:33:44,147 --> 00:33:44,827
[SPEAKER_02]: Remember that one?

800
00:33:44,887 --> 00:33:46,108
[SPEAKER_03]: No, I wasn't with them then.

801
00:33:46,128 --> 00:33:47,809
[SPEAKER_02]: Joey Sewell told that one yesterday.

802
00:33:47,829 --> 00:33:48,149
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

803
00:33:48,169 --> 00:33:50,850
[SPEAKER_02]: It was up in Cleveland, I guess.

804
00:33:50,870 --> 00:33:52,871
[SPEAKER_03]: We was coming back in Cleveland in the cab one day.

805
00:33:53,572 --> 00:33:56,013
[SPEAKER_03]: That's when our ball club was just trading everybody on us.

806
00:33:56,293 --> 00:33:57,554
[SPEAKER_03]: Everybody was trading.

807
00:33:57,574 --> 00:34:01,536
[SPEAKER_03]: When you'd see the morning, come to breakfast the next morning, shake hands with a different ball player.

808
00:34:02,276 --> 00:34:05,618
[SPEAKER_03]: Smead's coming back in the cab saying, ha, they won't trade old Pardner.

809
00:34:05,678 --> 00:34:07,899
[SPEAKER_03]: Old Pardner's got his place made here.

810
00:34:08,579 --> 00:34:09,420
[SPEAKER_03]: Got into the hotel.

811
00:34:10,450 --> 00:34:12,011
[SPEAKER_03]: Joe Barry, who said, hey, it's me.

812
00:34:12,031 --> 00:34:12,572
[SPEAKER_03]: Come here a minute.

813
00:34:13,132 --> 00:34:15,474
[SPEAKER_03]: Said, you've been traded to the St. Louis Browns.

814
00:34:17,376 --> 00:34:22,099
[SPEAKER_03]: And Bump Hadley and I in the same town, Cleveland, they were just trading ball players over.

815
00:34:22,780 --> 00:34:32,307
[SPEAKER_03]: Bump and I, the way we would do when we'd come into a hotel, one fellow would have the red cap, take the bags up the room, while the other one, I got him a milkshake or something like that.

816
00:34:33,308 --> 00:34:34,689
[SPEAKER_03]: And then the other one would come in.

817
00:34:35,310 --> 00:34:36,791
[SPEAKER_03]: But Bump went out that night.

818
00:34:37,201 --> 00:34:39,262
[SPEAKER_03]: to get his milk shake, and I took his bag up to him.

819
00:34:39,282 --> 00:34:44,043
