1018
00:44:06,950 --> 00:44:13,454
[SPEAKER_02]: What happened, was your knee again just the, did one thing happen there, or is it accumulation of the cartilage?

1019
00:44:13,534 --> 00:44:15,675
[SPEAKER_02]: Cartilage, and cartilage on both sides of it.

1020
00:44:15,955 --> 00:44:17,336
[SPEAKER_02]: That was in 36?

1021
00:44:17,836 --> 00:44:19,737
[SPEAKER_03]: No, that's a long time before then.

1022
00:44:19,777 --> 00:44:23,459
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know just now when it was.

1023
00:44:24,540 --> 00:44:26,701
[SPEAKER_03]: Sam Jones, get that quote Sam Jones made.

1024
00:44:27,121 --> 00:44:28,022
[SPEAKER_03]: He knows Sam.

1025
00:44:29,903 --> 00:44:30,443
[SPEAKER_03]: Didn't know him.

1026
00:44:36,735 --> 00:44:40,077
[SPEAKER_03]: You hear people talk about C&I dogs, they're wonderful.

1027
00:44:40,378 --> 00:44:42,899
[SPEAKER_03]: They take care of them.

1028
00:44:42,919 --> 00:44:47,102
[SPEAKER_03]: This is my fifth boxer.

1029
00:44:47,122 --> 00:44:53,326
[SPEAKER_03]: The reason people always ask me why do they have German Shepherds and C&I, why do so many people use them?

1030
00:44:54,067 --> 00:44:55,708
[SPEAKER_03]: That breeding division is Shepherds.

1031
00:44:56,429 --> 00:44:56,949
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why.

1032
00:44:57,269 --> 00:45:01,992
[SPEAKER_00]: We've got stacks and stacks of scrapbooks, but I just can't take them out.

1033
00:45:05,571 --> 00:45:06,552
[SPEAKER_02]: Who kept the scrapbooks?

1034
00:45:06,592 --> 00:45:09,613
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you keep them?

1035
00:45:09,954 --> 00:45:15,597
[SPEAKER_03]: She kept most of them, but somebody sent me two and I don't know yet who did it.

1036
00:45:16,017 --> 00:45:20,200
[SPEAKER_03]: Show him one of them, just let him look at it.

1037
00:45:20,260 --> 00:45:22,321
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't mean to look through it, just show him how it's shaped.

1038
00:45:24,012 --> 00:45:26,814
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, leave it alone, just leave it alone.

1039
00:45:27,474 --> 00:45:31,297
[SPEAKER_00]: Somebody left them at the hotel for me.

1040
00:45:32,758 --> 00:45:37,101
[SPEAKER_00]: I came in from the ballpark one day and they weren't there.

1041
00:45:38,321 --> 00:45:42,644
[SPEAKER_02]: It's so interesting how the wives often keep the scrapbooks for the husbands.

1042
00:45:42,684 --> 00:45:44,405
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, she's got all kinds.

1043
00:45:52,368 --> 00:45:55,029
[SPEAKER_02]: How about umpire relations?

1044
00:45:55,690 --> 00:45:58,431
[SPEAKER_02]: Good, good all of them.

1045
00:45:58,531 --> 00:46:01,672
[SPEAKER_03]: One put me out, I've never been put out, but two ball games.

1046
00:46:02,072 --> 00:46:05,454
[SPEAKER_03]: One I threw the ball out against the ground, I know the man's out at first.

1047
00:46:05,734 --> 00:46:12,777
[SPEAKER_03]: And Mr. Connelly was supervisor of umpires, and he told me later that he called that umpire down for that, said he was out there.

1048
00:46:13,197 --> 00:46:17,238
[SPEAKER_03]: Said that umpire missed that play and said he shouldn't have put you out of the game for throwing that ball against the ground.

1049
00:46:17,478 --> 00:46:19,219
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the first time I was ever put out of a game.

1050
00:46:19,299 --> 00:46:20,240
[SPEAKER_02]: Who was the umpire?

1051
00:46:20,520 --> 00:46:21,160
[SPEAKER_03]: Red Ornsby.

1052
00:46:21,959 --> 00:46:25,380
[SPEAKER_03]: And then we went over to Boston one day, and I had a broken thumb.

1053
00:46:25,620 --> 00:46:28,702
[SPEAKER_03]: I had it in a cast, and I was sitting up on the top step of the dugout.

1054
00:46:29,422 --> 00:46:32,163
[SPEAKER_03]: Somebody at the bench was yelling to Luke Cole, yelling, yelling.

1055
00:46:32,183 --> 00:46:34,024
[SPEAKER_03]: He looked over, and he said, you get out of here!

1056
00:46:40,346 --> 00:46:41,527
[SPEAKER_02]: Luke Cole.

1057
00:46:42,847 --> 00:46:45,568
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, McGowan, what did you think of Bill McGowan?

1058
00:46:45,588 --> 00:46:46,709
[SPEAKER_03]: Bill McGowan was a good umpire.

1059
00:46:46,729 --> 00:46:49,110
[SPEAKER_03]: Harry Geisel, you remember Harry Geisel?

1060
00:46:49,390 --> 00:46:50,010
[SPEAKER_03]: One of the best.

1061
00:46:50,662 --> 00:46:51,923
[SPEAKER_03]: One of the men, you know what he'd do?

1062
00:46:52,783 --> 00:46:57,266
[SPEAKER_03]: If you run up to Harry and start talking, say, now Harry, I know that man was out.

1063
00:46:57,606 --> 00:46:58,987
[SPEAKER_03]: Now so and so and so and so.

1064
00:46:59,027 --> 00:47:00,147
[SPEAKER_03]: He said, now listen, Jack.

1065
00:47:01,348 --> 00:47:02,388
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the way I called it.

1066
00:47:03,009 --> 00:47:04,129
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the way I saw it.

1067
00:47:04,690 --> 00:47:05,730
[SPEAKER_03]: And I'm not going to change it.

1068
00:47:05,870 --> 00:47:06,951
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, now, what are you going to do?

1069
00:47:07,972 --> 00:47:10,813
[SPEAKER_03]: The ones that jaw back at you, that's where the arguments start.

1070
00:47:11,033 --> 00:47:12,354
[SPEAKER_03]: When you jaw back at them, what?

1071
00:47:12,934 --> 00:47:14,235
[SPEAKER_03]: Bill Guthrie used to take them.

1072
00:47:14,255 --> 00:47:15,055
[SPEAKER_03]: You remember Bill Guthrie?

1073
00:47:15,075 --> 00:47:16,376
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I was going to ask you about Guthrie.

1074
00:47:19,413 --> 00:47:22,474
[SPEAKER_03]: I bagged a man out a second, I thought he was out, and I looked up.

1075
00:47:23,214 --> 00:47:25,195
[SPEAKER_00]: I said, that man was out!

1076
00:47:25,215 --> 00:47:26,576
[SPEAKER_03]: He said, no, it was the same.

1077
00:47:26,636 --> 00:47:28,056
[SPEAKER_03]: I said, well, it was close then!

1078
00:47:28,456 --> 00:47:30,697
[SPEAKER_03]: He said, listen, kiddo, there ain't no close ones.

1079
00:47:30,717 --> 00:47:32,918
[SPEAKER_03]: They either this or they that.

1080
00:47:33,318 --> 00:47:34,419
[SPEAKER_03]: You remember how he talked?

1081
00:47:34,939 --> 00:47:37,119
[SPEAKER_03]: An Italian dog said, either this or they that.

1082
00:47:38,600 --> 00:47:43,722
[SPEAKER_03]: So I kept my mouth shut from that old Bill I mean about, because I knew he was going to say, either this or that.

1083
00:47:44,682 --> 00:47:46,563
[SPEAKER_03]: Bill Summers was a great umpire.

1084
00:47:47,049 --> 00:47:47,969
[SPEAKER_03]: You remember Bill Summers?

1085
00:47:48,230 --> 00:47:49,970
[SPEAKER_02]: Big heavy settler, a little heavy settler.

1086
00:47:50,090 --> 00:47:51,151
[SPEAKER_03]: Right, big chet.

1087
00:47:51,291 --> 00:47:55,052
[SPEAKER_03]: I'll tell you another man used to be a good... George Moriarty was a good umpire.

1088
00:47:55,433 --> 00:47:56,173
[SPEAKER_03]: Billy Evans.

1089
00:47:56,453 --> 00:47:58,114
[SPEAKER_02]: Was Deneen still umpiring your time?

1090
00:47:58,134 --> 00:47:58,894
[SPEAKER_03]: Deneen.

1091
00:47:59,514 --> 00:48:01,595
[SPEAKER_03]: And what's that other one used to go around with him all the time?

1092
00:48:01,615 --> 00:48:02,095
[SPEAKER_03]: Hildebrand?

1093
00:48:02,756 --> 00:48:03,436
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't know.

1094
00:48:03,456 --> 00:48:07,297
[SPEAKER_03]: It was Deneen, Hildebrand, and three of them.

1095
00:48:07,598 --> 00:48:12,240
[SPEAKER_03]: Hildebrand, Deneen, and I just can't remember the other names of them.

1096
00:48:12,300 --> 00:48:13,620
[SPEAKER_02]: Roy Van Graafelen.

1097
00:48:13,680 --> 00:48:15,201
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, I remember Roy real well.

1098
00:48:15,221 --> 00:48:15,761
[SPEAKER_03]: Dick Allen.

1099
00:48:16,378 --> 00:48:18,119
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the one, Dineen and Nalin.

1100
00:48:18,339 --> 00:48:19,880
[SPEAKER_03]: They always worked together, looked like.

1101
00:48:19,920 --> 00:48:20,860
[SPEAKER_03]: Dineen and Nalin.

1102
00:48:21,621 --> 00:48:22,441
[SPEAKER_03]: They were good umpires.

1103
00:48:22,461 --> 00:48:23,241
[SPEAKER_03]: They were good fellas.

1104
00:48:23,922 --> 00:48:25,542
[SPEAKER_03]: I got along with them fine on the ball field.

1105
00:48:25,582 --> 00:48:28,244
[SPEAKER_03]: Of course, off the ball field, back then you never did see them.

1106
00:48:29,284 --> 00:48:35,287
[SPEAKER_03]: They passed a rule one time, you know, they thought there was something going on and they wouldn't let you go up and talk to people in the stand.

1107
00:48:36,167 --> 00:48:39,269
[SPEAKER_03]: A fella came up there one day and called me on me going, I wouldn't go.

1108
00:48:40,152 --> 00:48:43,134
[SPEAKER_03]: fellow from my hometown, and I went in there at the ballgame, and I saw him.

1109
00:48:43,454 --> 00:48:45,036
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, I want to ask you something.

1110
00:48:45,076 --> 00:48:45,776
[SPEAKER_03]: I said, what is it?

1111
00:48:46,277 --> 00:48:48,318
[SPEAKER_03]: He said, why wouldn't you come up and speak to me?

1112
00:48:48,959 --> 00:48:52,381
[SPEAKER_03]: I said, well, the president just made a real $500 fine for us to go up and shake hands.

1113
00:48:52,401 --> 00:48:53,742
[SPEAKER_03]: I said, I understand.

1114
00:48:54,122 --> 00:48:54,703
[SPEAKER_03]: That's right.

1115
00:48:54,763 --> 00:48:56,664
[SPEAKER_03]: I didn't see you until after the game.

1116
00:48:57,125 --> 00:48:57,685
[SPEAKER_03]: And he did.

1117
00:48:58,365 --> 00:49:04,050
[SPEAKER_03]: There was some gambling going on some way, and he thought there was something wrong.

1118
00:49:04,070 --> 00:49:06,071
[SPEAKER_03]: You remember when Judge Landis was commissioner?

1119
00:49:06,151 --> 00:49:07,852
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

1120
00:49:07,872 --> 00:49:08,533
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, what he said.

1121
00:49:09,224 --> 00:49:09,964
[SPEAKER_03]: It went that way.

1122
00:49:10,064 --> 00:49:11,265
[SPEAKER_03]: Did you ever meet the judge?

1123
00:49:11,365 --> 00:49:11,825
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, sir.

1124
00:49:12,025 --> 00:49:12,645
[SPEAKER_03]: Years ago.

1125
00:49:12,665 --> 00:49:14,486
[SPEAKER_03]: He used to come out to White Sox Park all the time.

1126
00:49:14,766 --> 00:49:15,966
[SPEAKER_03]: Remember that hat he used to wear?

1127
00:49:16,646 --> 00:49:21,088
[SPEAKER_03]: Used to wear that hat like a snozzled orange all the time.

1128
00:49:21,328 --> 00:49:23,448
[SPEAKER_02]: Did he talk to the players at all?

1129
00:49:23,829 --> 00:49:24,229
[SPEAKER_02]: Did he?

1130
00:49:24,329 --> 00:49:25,249
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, at the ballpark.

1131
00:49:25,269 --> 00:49:26,249
[SPEAKER_02]: Just speak to you, that was all.

1132
00:49:26,349 --> 00:49:27,510
[SPEAKER_02]: Just speak to you.

1133
00:49:27,570 --> 00:49:28,070
[SPEAKER_02]: That's all.

1134
00:49:28,110 --> 00:49:29,710
[SPEAKER_02]: That's all.

1135
00:49:29,730 --> 00:49:31,631
[SPEAKER_03]: There was some great umpires.

1136
00:49:31,671 --> 00:49:33,592
[SPEAKER_03]: There was some great players in baseball.

1137
00:49:33,612 --> 00:49:38,293
[SPEAKER_03]: Lord have mercy, way back from 27 through 40, I saw some good ballplayers.

1138
00:49:40,503 --> 00:49:43,944
[SPEAKER_03]: And there wasn't only great ballplayers, there were great men off the ball field.

1139
00:49:43,964 --> 00:49:47,225
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, a lot of people used to think ballplayers are roughnecks.

1140
00:49:47,905 --> 00:49:54,066
[SPEAKER_03]: And some fellas told me that before I went up there, before the college players started to come in, they were kind of roughnecks.

1141
00:49:54,106 --> 00:50:03,088
[SPEAKER_03]: But you know, after the college players got to go, they'd go to Springtree and go to these hotels and go to these ball clubs and get them to come stop at their hotel.

1142
00:50:03,808 --> 00:50:06,629
[SPEAKER_03]: But before that, somebody didn't want me there.

1143
00:50:08,613 --> 00:50:09,514
[SPEAKER_03]: Remember Tony Pyatt?

1144
00:50:10,034 --> 00:50:11,755
[SPEAKER_03]: Tony Pyatt, one of my best friends.

1145
00:50:11,775 --> 00:50:12,195
[SPEAKER_03]: Is that right?

1146
00:50:12,335 --> 00:50:14,716
[SPEAKER_03]: He's the one that helped get that knife for me in Chicago.

1147
00:50:15,777 --> 00:50:20,820
[SPEAKER_03]: See, I had a knife in Chicago in 58, I think.

1148
00:50:20,860 --> 00:50:21,500
[SPEAKER_03]: 48, 48.

1149
00:50:21,520 --> 00:50:23,221
[SPEAKER_03]: I had a knife there, yeah.

1150
00:50:23,441 --> 00:50:23,901
[SPEAKER_03]: Big knife.

1151
00:50:24,101 --> 00:50:25,182
[SPEAKER_02]: But you were living here then?

1152
00:50:25,362 --> 00:50:25,882
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, sir.

1153
00:50:26,383 --> 00:50:29,084
[SPEAKER_02]: I've lived here every winter since I started playing baseball.

1154
00:50:29,384 --> 00:50:30,445
[SPEAKER_02]: I talked to Tony Pyatt.

1155
00:50:30,485 --> 00:50:31,886
[SPEAKER_02]: He runs the big automobile dealership.

1156
00:50:31,906 --> 00:50:32,506
[SPEAKER_02]: That's right.

1157
00:50:32,806 --> 00:50:33,386
[SPEAKER_02]: That's right.

1158
00:50:33,727 --> 00:50:34,947
[SPEAKER_02]: He's got a heart condition.

1159
00:50:34,967 --> 00:50:35,468
[SPEAKER_03]: That's right.

1160
00:50:35,528 --> 00:50:36,228
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, I have too.

1161
00:50:36,288 --> 00:50:37,929
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm wearing my third pacemaker now.

1162
00:50:38,505 --> 00:50:42,408
[SPEAKER_03]: I got my third pacemaker just put in about a month ago, two months ago, third one.

1163
00:50:43,468 --> 00:50:46,950
[SPEAKER_03]: And they're doing wonders.

1164
00:50:47,511 --> 00:50:53,174
[SPEAKER_02]: He put on a night for Monty Stratton, or I guess he got that car from Monty Stratton when he had a night.

1165
00:50:53,475 --> 00:50:57,317
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, they gave money, I think, enough to buy a farm out there, too, in Greenville, Texas.

1166
00:50:58,238 --> 00:50:59,478
[SPEAKER_03]: He lives in Greenville, Texas.

1167
00:51:00,059 --> 00:51:01,219
[SPEAKER_03]: You ever go see Monty?

1168
00:51:01,400 --> 00:51:02,080
[SPEAKER_03]: No, I've never seen him.

1169
00:51:02,100 --> 00:51:02,941
[SPEAKER_03]: You ought to go see him.

1170
00:51:02,981 --> 00:51:04,001
[SPEAKER_03]: He's a good boy.

1171
00:51:04,482 --> 00:51:06,843
[SPEAKER_03]: There's never been a better fellow anywhere than Monty Stratton.

1172
00:51:07,567 --> 00:51:08,968
[SPEAKER_03]: had that terrible accident.

1173
00:51:09,448 --> 00:51:11,709
[SPEAKER_03]: And he was right in his prime.

1174
00:51:12,209 --> 00:51:14,810
[SPEAKER_03]: He had won, I think, 15 games a year before.

1175
00:51:15,810 --> 00:51:17,171
[SPEAKER_03]: And we were really counting on it.

1176
00:51:17,271 --> 00:51:18,031
[SPEAKER_03]: That had to happen.

1177
00:51:18,571 --> 00:51:24,093
[SPEAKER_03]: And the strangest thing, we were going to Chicago for an operation on my arm or something, and we stopped it.

1178
00:51:24,514 --> 00:51:26,494
[SPEAKER_03]: Been since Indiana, spent the night there.

1179
00:51:27,315 --> 00:51:29,155
[SPEAKER_03]: Next morning, I went down, went up the newsstand.

1180
00:51:29,195 --> 00:51:30,836
[SPEAKER_03]: I told them I'm on the Chicago Tribune.

1181
00:51:31,316 --> 00:51:33,537
[SPEAKER_03]: There's headlines that Monty Stratton had shot his leg.

1182
00:51:34,377 --> 00:51:34,477
[SPEAKER_03]: Ah!

1183
00:51:36,364 --> 00:51:38,286
[SPEAKER_03]: It was a bad, bad, bad trip.

1184
00:51:38,306 --> 00:51:46,511
[SPEAKER_03]: We'd get out there on that field and put on that wooden leg, a piece of batting practice, stick it out and catch the ground ball, and it was goody, goody, didn't hit, didn't hurt me.

1185
00:51:46,531 --> 00:51:48,953
[SPEAKER_03]: It hit, boop, it hit the bounce hole.

1186
00:51:49,353 --> 00:51:52,556
[SPEAKER_03]: And he'd come in the shower, leg was cut off about right there.

1187
00:51:53,216 --> 00:51:59,741
[SPEAKER_03]: Soap it up good, come hopping in there on that one leg, get you right up there, throw that soap bar.

1188
00:52:00,962 --> 00:52:03,043
[SPEAKER_03]: He was the one that took it.

1189
00:52:03,063 --> 00:52:03,603
[SPEAKER_03]: He took it.

1190
00:52:09,093 --> 00:52:14,296
[SPEAKER_03]: I saw Joe Sewell play ball down here at this ballpark.

1191
00:52:14,356 --> 00:52:16,697
[SPEAKER_03]: It was named after me years and years ago.

1192
00:52:17,918 --> 00:52:21,440
[SPEAKER_03]: He was playing with a town team called Eclectic.

1193
00:52:22,741 --> 00:52:25,162
[SPEAKER_03]: The final score of the game was 1-0.

1194
00:52:25,482 --> 00:52:26,583
[SPEAKER_03]: Eclectic beat Clanton.

1195
00:52:26,943 --> 00:52:28,204
[SPEAKER_03]: And that man hit the home run.

1196
00:52:28,564 --> 00:52:29,604
[SPEAKER_03]: Was a cross-hand hitter.

1197
00:52:30,045 --> 00:52:31,105
[SPEAKER_03]: First one I'd ever seen.

1198
00:52:31,706 --> 00:52:34,007
[SPEAKER_03]: And I still remember his name was Woodall.

1199
00:52:34,946 --> 00:52:37,568
[SPEAKER_03]: And that was in 1918 or something like that.

1200
00:52:37,588 --> 00:52:38,949
[SPEAKER_03]: But I still remember that ballgame.

1201
00:52:38,969 --> 00:52:41,851
[SPEAKER_03]: And I asked Joe not long ago if he remembered it.

1202
00:52:42,612 --> 00:52:43,933
[SPEAKER_03]: Sure boy, I do.

1203
00:52:43,973 --> 00:52:45,254
[SPEAKER_03]: I was in that ballgame.

1204
00:52:45,874 --> 00:52:46,715
[SPEAKER_03]: And he said, that's right.

1205
00:52:46,735 --> 00:52:47,595
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the man that hit it.

1206
00:52:47,615 --> 00:52:48,976
[SPEAKER_03]: He's a lawyer over here at Tallis.

1207
00:52:49,017 --> 00:52:49,517
[SPEAKER_03]: He was.

1208
00:52:50,237 --> 00:52:51,458
[SPEAKER_03]: He said, yes, I do.

1209
00:52:51,538 --> 00:52:52,159
[SPEAKER_03]: I was in that game.

1210
00:52:52,419 --> 00:52:55,021
[SPEAKER_03]: They were dressed, back then we didn't have any pastries.

1211
00:52:55,061 --> 00:52:58,604
[SPEAKER_03]: And they walked through town in red uniforms, bright red.

1212
00:52:59,424 --> 00:53:00,365
[SPEAKER_03]: And when they got hot,

1213
00:53:01,028 --> 00:53:02,449
[SPEAKER_03]: And those new uniforms started melting.

1214
00:53:02,469 --> 00:53:03,990
[SPEAKER_03]: They had red run all over them.

1215
00:53:04,390 --> 00:53:06,011
[SPEAKER_02]: I was looking about it, and you'd never see.

1216
00:53:06,251 --> 00:53:08,973
[SPEAKER_02]: Didn't the White Sox have a black uniform?

