01:09:40,093 --> 01:09:44,475
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like Sam Jones used to call Bing Miller, but we quit doing that.

1621
01:09:44,495 --> 01:09:48,437
[SPEAKER_03]: He used to call him Uncle Tom, called us the Dark Era.

1622
01:09:48,497 --> 01:09:51,818
[SPEAKER_03]: He even called Toe Fletcher, he used to burn us out.

1623
01:09:52,478 --> 01:09:53,639
[SPEAKER_03]: Q's Fletcher burned us out.

1624
01:09:53,659 --> 01:09:54,499
[SPEAKER_03]: You remember Art Fletcher?

1625
01:09:55,680 --> 01:09:56,200
[SPEAKER_03]: Fletcher?

1626
01:09:56,500 --> 01:09:57,661
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, for years.

1627
01:09:57,701 --> 01:09:59,501
[SPEAKER_03]: He could steal signs.

1628
01:10:00,102 --> 01:10:02,363
[SPEAKER_03]: And we had a boy with us, let me tell you about it.

1629
01:10:02,383 --> 01:10:04,183
[SPEAKER_03]: He could get signs from those pitches.

1630
01:10:04,403 --> 01:10:05,144
[SPEAKER_03]: Mervin Shea?

1631
01:10:05,584 --> 01:10:06,004
[SPEAKER_03]: Catcher.

1632
01:10:06,644 --> 01:10:07,845
[SPEAKER_03]: He was first base coach.

1633
01:10:10,483 --> 01:10:15,505
[SPEAKER_03]: He didn't use that picture, but he moved his hand just like Jimmy Dishon.

1634
01:10:16,966 --> 01:10:23,129
[SPEAKER_03]: Jimmy Dishon came to here.

1635
01:10:23,830 --> 01:10:25,350
[SPEAKER_03]: Remember Tommy Bridges?

1636
01:10:26,271 --> 01:10:29,372
[SPEAKER_03]: If Tommy Bridges went way up like that, it was a curveball.

1637
01:10:30,073 --> 01:10:33,254
[SPEAKER_02]: If he'd go short, he really gave it away.

1638
01:10:33,775 --> 01:10:36,656
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, they watch him, I'm telling you, they watch him like a hawk watching a chicken.

1639
01:10:37,347 --> 01:10:40,249
[SPEAKER_02]: Were there any spitball pitchers still around when you came up there?

1640
01:10:40,269 --> 01:10:40,990
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, sir.

1641
01:10:41,150 --> 01:10:48,956
[SPEAKER_03]: John Packers Quinn, that fellow Mitchell was with Cleveland, and there's a fellow named, uh... Kovaleski was gone then, huh?

1642
01:10:48,976 --> 01:10:50,137
[SPEAKER_03]: Kovaleski was gone.

1643
01:10:50,277 --> 01:10:51,057
[SPEAKER_03]: Red Faber.

1644
01:10:51,277 --> 01:10:51,618
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah.

1645
01:10:52,318 --> 01:10:58,663
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, I played behind Red Faber, and I was the second base, and some fellow hit a little soft ground ball right after I joined the club.

1646
01:10:58,723 --> 01:11:00,204
[SPEAKER_03]: Nobody told me about it.

1647
01:11:00,584 --> 01:11:04,107
[SPEAKER_03]: I caught that ball and started throwing it to the first and threw it up the upper deck of the stand.

1648
01:11:04,147 --> 01:11:05,208
[SPEAKER_03]: I caught that slick spot.

1649
01:11:05,602 --> 01:11:06,642
[SPEAKER_03]: Do it right straight up.

1650
01:11:06,762 --> 01:11:08,763
[SPEAKER_03]: From then on, everyone was playing.

1651
01:11:08,783 --> 01:11:12,043
[SPEAKER_03]: I said, Jack, I'm going to get you a handful of dirt every time.

1652
01:11:12,063 --> 01:11:14,304
[SPEAKER_03]: Those balls, so I got to get me a handful of dirt.

1653
01:11:16,404 --> 01:11:20,605
[SPEAKER_03]: When you catch a spit ball, is it that time?

1654
01:11:21,885 --> 01:11:23,366
[SPEAKER_03]: It might take too long here now.

1655
01:11:23,386 --> 01:11:24,006
[SPEAKER_03]: That's all right.

1656
01:11:24,026 --> 01:11:26,366
[SPEAKER_02]: We're having a great time in the water.

1657
01:11:26,426 --> 01:11:26,986
[SPEAKER_02]: It's fun.

1658
01:11:27,026 --> 01:11:28,167
[SPEAKER_02]: Here you are.

1659
01:11:28,767 --> 01:11:30,587
[SPEAKER_03]: Thank you, ma'am.

1660
01:11:30,627 --> 01:11:31,107
[SPEAKER_02]: Thank you, sweetie.

1661
01:11:31,547 --> 01:11:31,887
[SPEAKER_02]: Thank you.

1662
01:11:35,573 --> 01:11:37,154
[SPEAKER_02]: George Uley told me about it.

1663
01:11:37,214 --> 01:11:39,555
[SPEAKER_03]: One of the greatest pitchers of all times, I always thought.

1664
01:11:39,575 --> 01:11:40,695
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you bat against him?

1665
01:11:40,735 --> 01:11:41,196
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, sir.

1666
01:11:41,276 --> 01:11:41,996
[SPEAKER_03]: And a good hitter.

1667
01:11:42,676 --> 01:11:44,357
[SPEAKER_02]: I think he's proud of his hitting and his pitching.

1668
01:11:44,377 --> 01:11:45,598
[SPEAKER_03]: Man, he was a good hitter.

1669
01:11:45,618 --> 01:11:48,039
[SPEAKER_03]: He could hit that ball.

1670
01:11:48,079 --> 01:11:49,499
[SPEAKER_03]: He'd have been a great outfielder.

1671
01:11:50,060 --> 01:11:51,080
[SPEAKER_03]: He could hit that ball.

1672
01:11:51,200 --> 01:11:58,363
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, he was telling me about the 1920 World Series when the Indians were playing the Dodgers and Burley Grimes as a football pitcher.

1673
01:11:58,383 --> 01:11:58,744
[SPEAKER_02]: Right.

1674
01:11:59,244 --> 01:12:00,745
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I hit against Burley Grimes in spring, too.

1675
01:12:00,765 --> 01:12:03,846
[SPEAKER_02]: They used to spot that Brooklyn second baseman Jake Kilduff.

1676
01:12:04,549 --> 01:12:06,449
[SPEAKER_02]: Every so often, reach down and grab some dirt.

1677
01:12:06,569 --> 01:12:07,710
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, the spitter.

1678
01:12:08,450 --> 01:12:11,851
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why I said, on every pitch, I'd get dirt.

1679
01:12:11,911 --> 01:12:15,271
[SPEAKER_03]: If I just got it on the spitter, they'd know that's when it was coming.

1680
01:12:15,331 --> 01:12:17,592
[SPEAKER_03]: So I'd reach down and get some on every pitch.

1681
01:12:17,632 --> 01:12:19,212
[SPEAKER_03]: Fastball, curveball.

1682
01:12:19,312 --> 01:12:20,712
[SPEAKER_02]: You knew when the spitter was coming from there?

1683
01:12:20,732 --> 01:12:22,033
[SPEAKER_03]: Old Red Faber was a sight.

1684
01:12:22,713 --> 01:12:24,813
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I could see him slopping all over that.

1685
01:12:25,073 --> 01:12:26,634
[SPEAKER_02]: He used this old slip rail.

1686
01:12:26,834 --> 01:12:28,854
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, he slopped when he didn't throw the spitter.

1687
01:12:28,914 --> 01:12:29,854
[SPEAKER_02]: Just throw the batter off.

1688
01:12:29,874 --> 01:12:30,154
[SPEAKER_02]: Right.

1689
01:12:30,315 --> 01:12:31,215
[SPEAKER_02]: Just give the impression.

1690
01:12:31,255 --> 01:12:32,415
[SPEAKER_03]: Right, right.

1691
01:12:32,724 --> 01:12:33,584
[SPEAKER_02]: You couldn't do it every time.

1692
01:12:33,605 --> 01:12:38,087
[SPEAKER_03]: No, we had a way of telling them when he was going, you see what the catcher called for, where he's going.

1693
01:12:38,427 --> 01:12:41,149
[SPEAKER_03]: We would, we always went by the catcher signs.

1694
01:12:41,529 --> 01:12:43,510
[SPEAKER_03]: And what the catcher called for is what we played for.

1695
01:12:43,690 --> 01:12:45,311
[SPEAKER_03]: That's where you got to play out there on your toes.

1696
01:12:45,691 --> 01:12:54,836
[SPEAKER_03]: And you'd always, I've always said, and I still believe, that if you're sitting in the stand and you watch your outfielders, you don't even have to look at that picture.

1697
01:12:54,876 --> 01:12:59,039
[SPEAKER_03]: You can tell when that pitcher throws the ball by the outfielders coming up on the toes, getting ready to go.

1698
01:12:59,299 --> 01:13:01,500
[SPEAKER_03]: Infielders getting up on the toes, getting ready to go.

1699
01:13:02,079 --> 01:13:03,780
[SPEAKER_03]: You can tell when that ball is being thrown.

1700
01:13:03,860 --> 01:13:07,221
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, if you're just a spectator, you just notice that at some ball game.

1701
01:13:07,241 --> 01:13:08,601
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, you see outfielders.

1702
01:13:08,661 --> 01:13:11,862
[SPEAKER_03]: One of the hustling outfielders are the ones that come up and get ready to go.

1703
01:13:12,262 --> 01:13:16,283
[SPEAKER_03]: If you're up and ready to go, you've got to start on them.

1704
01:13:16,323 --> 01:13:19,544
[SPEAKER_02]: Can they see from out in the field the signals very well?

1705
01:13:19,664 --> 01:13:20,464
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no.

1706
01:13:20,484 --> 01:13:21,545
[SPEAKER_03]: You mean out in the outfield?

1707
01:13:21,565 --> 01:13:21,745
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

1708
01:13:22,245 --> 01:13:23,425
[SPEAKER_03]: No, they can't tell.

1709
01:13:23,845 --> 01:13:26,646
[SPEAKER_03]: Sometimes infielders give them signals.

1710
01:13:27,206 --> 01:13:29,027
[SPEAKER_03]: They have some sign to give to them.

1711
01:13:30,155 --> 01:13:32,495
[SPEAKER_03]: Or a hand behind the back, a hand on the shoulder, or something.

1712
01:13:32,515 --> 01:13:34,036
[SPEAKER_03]: Let them know what's going to be pitched.

1713
01:13:34,476 --> 01:13:37,296
[SPEAKER_03]: But I don't think that's too much help to an outfielder.

1714
01:13:37,656 --> 01:13:42,437
[SPEAKER_03]: Because before the ball games, you go over the lineup, and you know about which way you want to play them.

1715
01:13:42,477 --> 01:13:45,278
[SPEAKER_03]: Which way the manager wants you to play them, I should say.

1716
01:13:45,458 --> 01:13:48,578
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, but now I think it's where you want to play them.

1717
01:13:48,738 --> 01:13:49,138
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

1718
01:13:49,158 --> 01:13:50,278
[SPEAKER_03]: Players run the game now.

1719
01:13:51,219 --> 01:13:52,419
[SPEAKER_03]: That's what DeRosa said.

1720
01:13:52,479 --> 01:13:52,979
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't know.

1721
01:13:52,999 --> 01:13:54,439
[SPEAKER_03]: DeRosa said, players run the game.

1722
01:13:54,459 --> 01:13:55,519
[SPEAKER_03]: He said, the manager's got a chance.

1723
01:13:55,539 --> 01:13:57,340
[SPEAKER_03]: He said, the ball players make more money than the manager.

1724
01:13:57,640 --> 01:13:59,160
[SPEAKER_03]: So they tell us what to do.

1725
01:14:00,890 --> 01:14:02,551
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you back up when Ruth came to bat?

1726
01:14:03,191 --> 01:14:03,411
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

1727
01:14:03,491 --> 01:14:05,092
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, I'd get back on the edge of the grass.

1728
01:14:05,112 --> 01:14:06,332
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, on the edge of the grass.

1729
01:14:06,392 --> 01:14:07,132
[SPEAKER_03]: I wouldn't get out now.

1730
01:14:07,152 --> 01:14:08,033
[SPEAKER_03]: I'd feel like I'd get back on the edge.

1731
01:14:08,453 --> 01:14:11,614
[SPEAKER_03]: And we never pulled that big shift around like they used to pull.

1732
01:14:11,634 --> 01:14:14,315
[SPEAKER_02]: Could Ruth hit the left field at all, or didn't he ever?

1733
01:14:14,815 --> 01:14:15,255
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't know.

1734
01:14:15,295 --> 01:14:16,016
[SPEAKER_03]: I know one thing.

1735
01:14:16,196 --> 01:14:18,057
[SPEAKER_03]: He'd hit some of the highest ones he's ever hit.

1736
01:14:18,417 --> 01:14:21,518
[SPEAKER_03]: Ooh, you'd go round and round and round before we'd come down.

1737
01:14:22,678 --> 01:14:29,801
[SPEAKER_03]: And in Comiskey Park, if the wind's blowing just right, if that ball would get above the stands, if you take the winds coming from that direction,

1738
01:14:30,312 --> 01:14:31,372
[SPEAKER_03]: It blows back in the fire.

1739
01:14:31,392 --> 01:14:33,673
[SPEAKER_03]: If you didn't stay with it, you wouldn't get it.

1740
01:14:35,054 --> 01:14:36,055
[SPEAKER_03]: Lots of times.

1741
01:14:36,115 --> 01:14:39,936
[SPEAKER_03]: I've seen balls that you sweat are going to be foul balls, and they land fire.

1742
01:14:41,137 --> 01:14:47,600
[SPEAKER_03]: But you always, before they, between innings, we did, all the time, look back to see what our flag was doing.

1743
01:14:48,380 --> 01:14:53,702
[SPEAKER_03]: Because if the wind changes, comes off of that lake, you can change your ball game right quick.

1744
01:14:56,804 --> 01:14:58,984
[SPEAKER_02]: What a fascinating career it's been.

1745
01:14:59,145 --> 01:14:59,585
[SPEAKER_02]: Thanks, sir.

1746
01:15:01,473 --> 01:15:02,273
[SPEAKER_03]: I had a lot of fun.

1747
01:15:02,474 --> 01:15:02,994
[SPEAKER_03]: I loved it.

1748
01:15:03,294 --> 01:15:12,979
[SPEAKER_03]: I didn't just didn't play for the money because we didn't get much money but I played for the fun and I loved all the ball players and I hope they loved me.

1749
01:15:13,820 --> 01:15:20,323
[SPEAKER_02]: What would you do in the uh off the field as recreation or entertainment?

1750
01:15:20,783 --> 01:15:23,605
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh when Dykes got to be manager he let's go play golf.

1751
01:15:24,433 --> 01:15:26,914
[SPEAKER_03]: Up until then, the managers wouldn't let you play golf.

1752
01:15:26,934 --> 01:15:29,316
[SPEAKER_03]: They were afraid you'd interfere with your swing at baseball.

1753
01:15:29,876 --> 01:15:32,217
[SPEAKER_03]: But Dax is so crazy about golf, he lets you play.

1754
01:15:32,237 --> 01:15:37,860
[SPEAKER_03]: We didn't even have a tournament between the White Sox and the Cubs golf tournament.

1755
01:15:38,421 --> 01:15:40,262
[SPEAKER_03]: And some of the boys would go to racetrack.

1756
01:15:40,302 --> 01:15:41,382
[SPEAKER_03]: I never cared much about it.

1757
01:15:41,442 --> 01:15:42,263
[SPEAKER_03]: I didn't bet on them.

1758
01:15:42,903 --> 01:15:44,344
[SPEAKER_02]: I'd go out there and watch a lot of them.

1759
01:15:44,424 --> 01:15:45,024
[SPEAKER_02]: Off season.

1760
01:15:48,666 --> 01:15:49,567
[SPEAKER_02]: race track did he?

1761
01:15:49,767 --> 01:15:55,270
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, he's afraid, I think he's kind of afraid of getting mixed up with the cameras.

1762
01:15:55,430 --> 01:15:58,732
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't quote this Bill Sissel, that's what he did.

1763
01:15:58,812 --> 01:16:03,114
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't understand it for a while.

1764
01:16:04,455 --> 01:16:06,736
[SPEAKER_02]: I knew you were going to mention that name.

1765
01:16:07,336 --> 01:16:09,738
[SPEAKER_02]: Maybe just tell me about him.

1766
01:16:09,758 --> 01:16:10,798
[SPEAKER_02]: Stevenson or somebody.

1767
01:16:11,438 --> 01:16:12,819
[SPEAKER_02]: Or maybe it was Greenfield.

1768
01:16:13,059 --> 01:16:14,180
[SPEAKER_03]: He was over there with Stevie.

1769
01:16:14,620 --> 01:16:15,501
[SPEAKER_03]: He was over there with the Cub.

1770
01:16:15,541 --> 01:16:16,021
[SPEAKER_03]: That's right.

1771
01:16:16,461 --> 01:16:17,862
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, he could hit that ball.

1772
01:16:18,883 --> 01:16:28,706
[SPEAKER_03]: And the funny, the strangest thing, now as long as he played ball, and as smart as he was, I can't never did understand why when he went on to St. Louis Browns he'd always make them take a strike.

1773
01:16:29,947 --> 01:16:31,647
[SPEAKER_03]: He didn't go up to play, they had to take a strike.

1774
01:16:32,628 --> 01:16:36,809
[SPEAKER_03]: Dick Kaufman, a boy from up here in North Alabama, pull a good one on him though.

1775
01:16:37,309 --> 01:16:42,591
[SPEAKER_03]: I said, Hornsby made a rule, said no smoking and no newspapers in the clubhouse.

1776
01:16:43,131 --> 01:16:48,033
[SPEAKER_03]: So they went to Yankee Stadium and they got a big hall outside the clubhouse door there.

1777
01:16:48,575 --> 01:16:49,895
[SPEAKER_03]: All the trunks were lined up out there.

1778
01:16:49,955 --> 01:16:56,897
[SPEAKER_03]: He said one day, once we came in, he caught me sitting out there, just had a jockstrap on, had a cigarette, and a newspaper in his hand.

1779
01:16:57,477 --> 01:16:59,298
[SPEAKER_03]: He said, well, you said not in the clubhouse.

1780
01:16:59,318 --> 01:17:04,299
[SPEAKER_03]: He said, I'm sitting out here now.

1781
01:17:04,319 --> 01:17:10,041
[SPEAKER_02]: But he blew up, huh?

1782
01:17:10,521 --> 01:17:12,201
[SPEAKER_02]: I've asked a number of people about that.

1783
01:17:12,241 --> 01:17:16,903
[SPEAKER_02]: He seems to be such a hard man to get on with, from what I read.

1784
01:17:16,923 --> 01:17:18,003
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I never played under him.

1785
01:17:18,587 --> 01:17:25,468
[SPEAKER_03]: But off of the field, I mean, in an opposing team, I was found to be clean and a good fellow.

1786
01:17:25,548 --> 01:17:28,109
[SPEAKER_03]: But of course, that was after his playing days, most of when I was with him.

1787
01:17:29,049 --> 01:17:32,950
[SPEAKER_03]: I saw him play when he was with the Johnson, somebody way back.

1788
01:17:33,210 --> 01:17:35,630
[SPEAKER_03]: I was just a kid coming up in 27.

1789
01:17:40,211 --> 01:17:43,392
[SPEAKER_03]: Travis Jackson, he was a shortstop for the Johnson.

1790
01:17:43,432 --> 01:17:45,973
[SPEAKER_03]: Freddie Lindstrom, Bill Terry was the first baseman.

1791
01:17:46,073 --> 01:17:46,833
[SPEAKER_03]: Hugh Crites.

1792
01:17:48,437 --> 01:17:53,118
[SPEAKER_03]: Andy Cohen was the second baby, and old Big Shanny Hogan was one of the catchers.

1793
01:17:53,238 --> 01:17:54,719
[SPEAKER_03]: And like you said, Eddie Rouse.

1794
01:17:54,879 --> 01:17:58,220
[SPEAKER_03]: Did you play against George Sisler at all when he was still with the Browns?

1795
01:17:58,240 --> 01:17:58,560
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, sir.

1796
01:17:58,580 --> 01:18:01,641
[SPEAKER_03]: George Sisler came to the Washington Club.

1797
01:18:01,841 --> 01:18:02,761
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, did he?

1798
01:18:02,901 --> 01:18:04,641
[SPEAKER_03]: He had sinus trouble when he came to us.

1799
01:18:05,082 --> 01:18:06,542
[SPEAKER_03]: He and Judge split first base.

1800
01:18:06,602 --> 01:18:10,923
[SPEAKER_03]: One played one for a while, and the other one would play when they were both going out.

1801
01:18:10,943 --> 01:18:12,504
[SPEAKER_03]: Remember George had sinus trouble?

1802
01:18:13,344 --> 01:18:14,564
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, he had eye trouble one year.

1803
01:18:14,584 --> 01:18:14,904
[SPEAKER_02]: That's right.

1804
01:18:14,924 --> 01:18:15,604
[SPEAKER_02]: It was sinus.

1805
01:18:15,624 --> 01:18:16,625
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why he had to quit.

1806
01:18:16,905 --> 01:18:17,805
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why he had to quit.

1807
01:18:18,105 --> 01:18:20,046
[SPEAKER_02]: Then he came back and hit 400 or something.

1808
01:18:20,466 --> 01:18:23,068
[SPEAKER_03]: He is a fine, he's a perfect gentleman, too.

1809
01:18:23,288 --> 01:18:23,808
[SPEAKER_03]: He was.

1810
01:18:24,648 --> 01:18:29,191
[SPEAKER_03]: You know these players when you're on the club with them, how they live off the field and how they live on the field.

1811
01:18:29,491 --> 01:18:31,772
[SPEAKER_03]: Somebody drank a beer once in a while, ain't nothing wrong with that.

1812
01:18:31,872 --> 01:18:34,934
[SPEAKER_02]: There's a pretty close camaraderie and spirit among them.

1813
01:18:34,974 --> 01:18:35,534
[SPEAKER_02]: I think so.

1814
01:18:36,014 --> 01:18:37,395
